m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Valine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: The network synthesizes L-valine from simple C1 and C2 feedstocks (CO2, HCN, NH3, CH4) in a geochemically plausible, multi-environment process. Central intermediates such as \u03b1-ketoisovaleric acid and isobutyraldehyde are constructed via Strecker, Reductive Amination, and Aldol pathways, making use of mineral-catalyzed carbon fixation (e.g., pyruvate synthesis), followed by amination in both hydrothermal and surface environments. Multiple routes leverage hydrothermal and surface minerals, with wet-dry cycles, and prebiotic pools to promote both carbon\u2013carbon bond formation and nitrogen incorporation, ultimately converging at valine through diverse chemical logic.\n\nMOLECULES (18 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2 (hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_004: CH4 (methane) [starting]\n  - mol_005: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_006: CH2O (formaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_007: CH3COCOOH (pyruvic acid) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: CH3CH(OH)COOH (lactic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_009: CH3COCHO (acetone) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: CH3CH2CHO (propanal) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: CH3CH(CH3)COOH (isovaleric acid) [intermediate]\